    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/byzas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzas.jpg" alt="Byzas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/byzas">Byzas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coinage_with_Byzas_2nd_3rd_century_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Imaging, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Byzantium was founded by Greek colonists from Megara, traditionally in 667 BCE, although ancient chronographers also gave dates around 660 BCE. Later legend named its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzas">Byzas</a> and told that the Delphic oracle had directed the settlers to build opposite the land of the blind. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports a related saying of the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megabazus">Megabazus</a>, who called the people of Chalcedon blind because they had settled on the Asian shore seventeen years earlier and ignored the far better site facing them. The colonists, perhaps joined by settlers from other Greek cities, occupied the triangular promontory between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a>, the Bosporus and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>, with their acropolis on the site of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>. The position offered everything a trading city could want: a deep, sheltered harbour in the Golden Horn, currents that carried shipping and migrating fish towards its shore, and command of the Bosporus, the only sea route between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Its weakness was the hinterland, which belonged to Thracian peoples who raided its fields and at times extracted tribute. In its first century Byzantium was a modest colony, one of several Megarian foundations around the straits, including Chalcedon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selymbria">Selymbria</a>, and it kept Megarian institutions, cults and the Doric dialect of its mother city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/athens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="Athens" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_Acropolis_at_Daybreak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Parlette, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Byzantium's wealth came from the sea. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, in a famous description of the city, explains how the currents of the Bosporus brought ships to its harbours rather than to Chalcedon opposite, and how the city stood astride the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> trade, which supplied the Greek world with slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, honey, wax and salted fish as well as grain. The seasonal migration of tuna and bonito through the straits made fishing a major industry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, in the <em>Politics</em>, names Byzantium as a city where fishermen formed a large part of the common people. The Byzantines also farmed the fertile land of their Thracian territory, though Polybius stresses that Thracian raids made its harvests insecure. The city's position allowed it to levy dues on passing ships, a source of revenue that its rulers, and at times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, tried to exploit. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> coins of the fourth century BCE showed a cow standing on a dolphin, probably alluding to the myth of Io, who crossed the strait in the form of a cow. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Byzantium controlled the Hieron, a sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> Ourios at the Black Sea entrance of the Bosporus where ships waited for favourable winds. The pseudo-Aristotelian <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oeconomica">Oeconomica</a></em> describes the expedients, including sales of public land, by which the Byzantines raised money in times of crisis.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Byzantium's walls made it hard to take, but its value drew every great power of the region. Around 513 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> crossed the Bosporus near the city on a bridge of boats, and Byzantium came under Persian rule. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>, and when the revolt collapsed its people fled to Mesembria on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, while a Phoenician fleet burned the city in 493 BCE. In 478 BCE the Greek fleet under the Spartan regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> captured Byzantium from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>; his arrogance there helped drive the allies to accept Athenian leadership. Byzantium revolted against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> in 440 BCE, and again in 411 BCE in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a> recovered it after a siege in 408 BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> took it back for Sparta in 405 BCE. The city's most celebrated feat was its resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>, who besieged it in 340–339 BCE. Aided by Athenian fleets under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chares-of-athens">Chares</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocion">Phocion</a> and by Chios, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and Kos, Byzantium held out and Philip withdrew. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-tylis">Kingdom of Tylis</a>, settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> after 279 BCE, extorted tribute that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> says rose to 80 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> a year. To pay it Byzantium imposed tolls on Black Sea shipping in 220 BCE, provoking a short war with Rhodes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prusias-i-of-bithynia">Prusias I of Bithynia</a>, after which it abandoned the tolls.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger.jpg" alt="Cyrus the Younger" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger">Cyrus the Younger</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_an_anonymous_Satrap_IONIA,_Phokaia._Circa_478-387_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Byzantium was a self-governing polis with a council and a citizen assembly, and its institutions, calendar and cults followed those of Megara. Its constitution shifted with the balance of power around it: democratic government generally prevailed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> was dominant, and oligarchic factions when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> was. After 405 BCE the Spartan governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clearchus-of-heraclea">Clearchus</a> ruled the city so harshly that Sparta itself removed him, and he went on to lead Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger">Cyrus the Younger</a>. As a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> Byzantium paid one of the highest tribute assessments in the Hellespontine district, reflecting its wealth. In 390 BCE the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrasybulus-of-miletus">Thrasybulus</a> restored democratic government and imposed a tithe on ships leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Byzantium was among the first members of the Second Athenian League in 378 BCE, but broke away with Chios, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and Kos in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-357-355-bce">Social War</a> of 357–355 BCE. Inscriptions and literary sources indicate that the city's chief eponymous official bore the title <em>hieromnemon</em>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> the city conducted its own diplomacy with kings and leagues, hired troops, signed treaties and, from the second century BCE, allied itself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Under Roman supremacy it kept the status of a free city with its own laws, though it paid tribute, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a>'s correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> later shows Roman governors scrutinising its finances.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantinople.jpg" alt="Constantinople" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_Constantinople-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cplakidas, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Byzantium's lasting importance lies above all in its site. The qualities <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> analysed, a defensible promontory, a superb harbour and control of the passage between two seas, led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> to refound the city as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the east for more than eleven centuries and afterwards of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Through it the name of the small Megarian colony passed to an entire civilisation, for early modern scholars, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieronymus-wolf">Hieronymus Wolf</a> in the sixteenth century, called the medieval Eastern Roman state the Byzantine Empire. In antiquity Byzantium mattered as the guardian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> route on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> cities depended for grain, which is why Athens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> fought for influence over it. The city produced notable scholars and engineers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes-of-byzantium">Aristophanes of Byzantium</a>, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a> around 200 BCE, edited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and other poets and is credited with developing the system of Greek accents, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byzantium">Philo of Byzantium</a> wrote on mechanics and siegecraft. Its defence against Philip II became a celebrated episode, and a later legend connected the crescent on the city's coins with the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecate">Hecate</a>, said to have lit the night sky to reveal a Macedonian attack, though the story is not attested in contemporary sources.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tacitus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tacitus.jpg" alt="Tacitus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien-_Parlament-Tacitus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pe-Jo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Byzantium did not fall in war; it was absorbed gradually into the Roman order. The tribute paid to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> of Tylis had strained its finances in the third century BCE, and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> limited its room to manoeuvre. From the second century BCE it sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, envoys reminded the Senate in 53 CE of the help their city had given to Roman commanders in wars against the Macedonian kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristonicus">Aristonicus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> responded by remitting their tribute for five years. As a free and allied city Byzantium kept its laws and magistrates, but real power lay with Roman governors and armies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> records that Vespasian took away the freedom of Byzantium, along with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, Lycia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a>, and around 73 CE it was incorporated into a Roman province. The city remained a prosperous port. In 193 CE it supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pescennius-niger">Pescennius Niger</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> and endured a long siege, after which Severus demolished its walls and reduced its status, though he is said to have later begun its rebuilding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>, having defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> nearby in 324 CE, refounded Byzantium on a far larger scale and dedicated it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 330 CE, beginning the city's second life as an imperial capital.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Byzantium matters as the Greek city that first exploited one of the most strategic sites on earth: from its Megarian founding it controlled the passage between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, withstood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>, and gave its name and its location to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and the Byzantine Empire.</em></p>

